title ALES117 — Lotus's stereo_cut, the foundational span-remove carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep
prompt recur the itinerary Lindy-first crux-first; after ALES116 sent, take the next agent-doable Lotus lap, self-approve, prove GREEN, send as its own signed round; STOP at custody gates
think ALES116 carried the general span-edit (replace) into stereo. Yet the family's root primitive — ALES2's cut, remove a span and close the gap — had no stereo twin of its own. Chosen crux-first because cut is the base every destructive stereo span-edit builds from: crop keeps the complement of what cut removes, move is a cut followed by a splice. Lifting cut into the StereoClip gives every future stereo span-edit its base gesture — circle a span on the master, delete it, both speakers in lockstep — so naming the base twin first is the move that opens the rest of the recommended stereo family (stereo_crop, stereo_move follow as its readings).
think Shape: stereo_cut(sc, at, count) runs ALES2's proven mono cut on each channel with the same at and count, no new primitive. The stereo discipline is validate-both-before-either, and here it is simpler than ALES116's replace: mono cut can fault ONLY with BadRange (a span outside the samples), and both channels enter equal-length (the defining StereoClip invariant), so the shared span is checked ONCE against the common length up front (start against len, count against the remainder, without underflow). Once that check passes, each mono cut is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits, so the channels can never desynchronise.
think Four laws: the STEREO CUT law (left equals mono cut(left, at, count) and right equals mono cut(right, at, count), each byte for byte, proven side by side over one anchor with differing per-channel samples, plus the concrete head-plus-tail); the BALANCE / INVARIANT law (left.len == right.len after, proven mid/head/tail); the ATOMICITY law (a refusal — a start past the samples, a count past the remainder — leaves BOTH channels untouched and still balanced, the check landing before either mutates); the DEGENERATE / SHRINK law (count 0 the identity on both channels, a full-length cut empties both, the length non-increasing because cut only drops samples).
obs GREEN on the first pass — no build red. All four laws pass on metal. The teaching crux is that cut's single fault mode (BadRange only, never ClipFull since cut never grows) makes the validate-both discipline a single shared check — simpler than ALES116's replace, which also had to bound a payload and guard headroom. Reuses ALES2's cut and EditError whole, invents no primitive and no error. >=2 positive-invariant asserts per fn (enter-balanced, leave-balanced, plus the span guards); width-check exit 0; TAME style bans clean, ratchet zero-assert count unchanged at 11. No custody gate touched — two pure-Rye local i16 Clips, siloed to lotus/.
loom witness=ales_stereo_cut_witness green=1 laws=4 rung=ALES117
file lotus/stereo_cut.rye the ALES117 rung — stereo_cut, ALES2's mono cut lifted to the StereoClip, validate-both-before-either (one shared BadRange check), four laws proven in-file
file tools/ales_stereo_cut_witness.rish the on-metal witness — build, selftest, GREEN-line assert, three-lens say head
file active-designing/20260815-015354_fill-ales117-lotus-stereo-cut.md the self-approved design read for the rung
file lotus/README.md front door synced — Status-line short entry and the long per-rung paragraph both carry ALES117, stamp refreshed
recommend keep-going the stereo edit family continues (stereo_crop as the keep-twin of ALES111 and stereo_move as the relocate-twin of ALES113 are the natural next rungs, each the mono op run per channel with the validate-both discipline); the loop stays Lindy-first crux-first; STOP remains at REMEMBER's custody gates
